return A.c(A.nM(m,"how_to_play",'<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n<head>\n  <meta charset="UTF-8">\n  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">\n  <title>T-G-Apps Bridge How to Play</title>\n  <style>\n    body {\n      margin: 0;\n      padding: 16px;\n      background: #f6f1e4;\n      color: #111827;\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n      font-size: 16px;\n      line-height: 1.45;\n    }\n\n    .tgapps-policy-page {\n      width: 100%;\n      max-width: 900px;\n      margin: 0 auto;\n    }\n\n    .hero,\n    .card {\n      display: block;\n      width: auto;\n      margin: 0 0 14px 0;\n      padding: 14px;\n      border-radius: 12px;\n      background: #ffffff;\n      border: 1px solid #D4AF37;\n    }\n\n    .hero {\n      background: #063a25;\n      color: #ffffff;\n    }\n\n    .badge {\n      display: none;\n    }\n\n    h1, h2, h3 {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 0;\n      color: #063a25;\n    }\n\n    .hero h1,\n    .hero h2 {\n      color: #ffd966;\n    }\n\n    p {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 0;\n    }\n\n    ul {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 22px;\n      padding: 0;\n    }\n\n    li {\n      margin-bottom: 5px;\n    }\n\n    a {\n      color: #0b63ce;\n      word-break: break-word;\n    }\n\n    .footer {\n      text-align: center;\n      color: #ffffff;\n      background: #063a25;\n      padding: 12px;\n      border-radius: 12px;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class="tgapps-policy-page">\n    <section class="hero">\n      <h1>T-G-Apps LLC</h1>\n      <h2>T-G-Apps Bridge How to Play</h2>\n      <p class="subtitle">Contract bridge: bid for a contract, then take the tricks you promised.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>1. Goal</h3>\n      <p>Four players play in two partnerships: North and South against East and West. Player 1 sits South, and your partner, Player 3, sits North. Each deal has an auction, where the sides compete to name the contract, and then the play of 13 tricks. The side with more points after the last deal wins.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>2. Setup Options</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Players: </strong>each player has a name and a type. Player 1 is always a person; tap the Type button to switch any other player between Human and Robot. If more than one person plays, pass the phone when the game asks you to.</li>\n        <li><strong>Number of deals: </strong>4, 8 or 12.</li>\n        <li><strong>Robot skill: </strong>Beginner, Intermediate or Expert. All robots play at the same level.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bidding system: </strong>Standard American, with five-card majors, weak twos and a strong 2\u2663. All four players, and the hints, use the same system. You can choose the 1NT opening range (15\u201317, 12\u201314 or 16\u201318) and the way to ask for aces (RKCB 1430, RKCB 3014 or Blackwood), and turn these conventions on or off: Stayman, Jacoby transfers, Jacoby 2NT, splinter bids, negative doubles, Michaels and Unusual 2NT, and weak jump overcalls.</li>\n        <li><strong>Card play agreements: </strong>leads from a long suit (fourth best, or third and fifth), the lead from ace-king (ace or king), and signals (standard or upside-down).</li>\n        <li><strong>Show hints: </strong>on or off. Turn hints off to test your own skill.</li>\n        <li><strong>I play both hands: </strong>when your robot partner wins the contract, you play both hands. Turn this off to let the robot play the contract while you are the dummy.</li>\n        <li><strong>Music: </strong>Background music on or off, and its volume.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>3. The Auction</h3>\n      <p>Starting with the dealer, each player in turn makes a call:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>A bid: </strong>a level from 1 to 7 and a strain: clubs, diamonds, hearts, spades or no trump, in that order from lowest to highest. A bid of 4\u2660 promises to take 6 + 4 = 10 tricks with spades as trumps. Each bid must be higher than the last.</li>\n        <li><strong>Pass.</strong></li>\n        <li><strong>Double </strong>an opponent\'s bid, which raises the stakes, or <strong>Redouble</strong> after your side\'s bid is doubled.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>In the bidding box, tap a level, then a strain. The auction ends after three passes in a row; the last bid is the contract. If all four players pass, nobody scores the deal.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>4. The Play</h3>\n      <p>The declarer is the player on the winning side who first bid the contract\'s strain. The player to declarer\'s left leads the first card; then declarer\'s partner, the dummy, lays their cards face up, and declarer plays both hands.</p>\n      <p>You must follow the suit led if you can. The highest trump wins the trick, or the highest card of the suit led if no trump was played. The winner leads the next trick.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>5. Scoring</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Trick points: </strong>clubs and diamonds 20 a trick, hearts and spades 30, no trump 40 for the first trick and 30 for the rest. Doubling or redoubling multiplies these.</li>\n        <li><strong>Bonuses: </strong>a contract worth 100 trick points or more is a game: 300 extra, or 500 when vulnerable. Otherwise the part score bonus is 50. A small slam (6) adds 500 or 750, a grand slam (7) 1000 or 1500.</li>\n        <li><strong>Going down: </strong>each trick short gives the defenders 50 (100 when vulnerable), more when doubled.</li>\n        <li><strong>Vulnerability </strong>changes each deal: nobody, then North-South, then East-West, then both.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>6. Hints and Robots</h3>\n      <p>Press Hint for advice on your call or your card. The recommended button or card is outlined in white. Hints always give the advice of the Expert robot.</p>\n      <p>Beginner robots bid by the system\'s rules and play by simple rules of thumb, such as drawing trumps, leading from sequences and playing low when partner is winning. Intermediate robots also use the auction to judge where the unseen cards are. Expert robots also think through many possible deals before a close bid and before each card.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>7. Games History</h3>\n      <p>Every finished game is saved with each side\'s final score. Games History lists the newest games first.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- tgapps-room-how-to-play 8 -->\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>Responsible Play</h3>\n      <p>T-G-Apps Bridge is an entertainment game. It does not offer real-money gambling, prizes, wagers or cash-out functionality.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <p class="footer">\xa9 2026 T-G-Apps LLC. All rights reserved.</p>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n',"tgapps_bridge_engine"),$async$LG)
